Free plan and how to learn it anyway.

40
00:02:46,081 --> 00:02:50,446
Tag rulesets. Why: a release tag that
moves is a supply-chain problem.

41
00:02:50,486 --> 00:02:54,410
A tag is a name for one commit,
and it is not expected to move.

42
00:02:54,450 --> 00:02:58,122
Whoever fetched the old tag has
one commit under that name.

43
00:02:58,162 --> 00:03:03,156
Whoever fetches tomorrow gets another.
And a build that resolves the tag by name

44
00:03:03,196 --> 00:03:06,742
ships whichever it gets. You
met this in the tags videos.

45
00:03:07,082 --> 00:03:12,119
What: on GitHub, a tag ruleset targeting
v* with three rules: restrict updates,

46
00:03:12,159 --> 00:03:17,260
restrict deletions and block force pushes.
Creation stays open, so a release can

47
00:03:17,300 --> 00:03:22,659
still be tagged. If only the release automation
may tag, add restrict creations with

48
00:03:22,699 --> 00:03:24,908
a bypass entry for that automation.

49
00:03:25,248 --> 00:03:30,635
How it works is the mechanism from two
videos ago, applied to another namespace: a

50
00:03:30,675 --> 00:03:34,937
tag push is a ref update with an
old ID, a new ID and a ref name.

51
00:03:34,977 --> 00:03:38,445
A moved tag is an update
where both IDs are non-zero.

52
00:03:38,785 --> 00:03:43,821
An outdated-advice note: "Tag protection
rules" under the repository settings were

53
00:03:43,861 --> 00:03:48,092
retired on the thirtieth of August
2024 and migrated to tag rulesets.

54
00:03:48,132 --> 00:03:52,920
And do not confuse either with the
immutable release from video 130, a second,

55
00:03:52,960 --> 00:03:56,881
different mechanism, which locks
the tag of a published release.

56
00:03:57,221 --> 00:04:00,657
The target pattern needs the
same care as for branches.

57
00:04:00,697 --> 00:04:05,712
v* matches every tag that begins with a
v, including ones that are not versions.

58
00:04:06,052 --> 00:04:10,994
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper:
write three tag names that v* matches, and

59
00:04:11,034 --> 00:04:14,731
make one of them something that
is not a version. I'll wait.

60
00:04:19,571 --> 00:04:26,045
Here are mine: v1.0.0, v2, and version-notes.
Keep the third one in mind for the

61
00:04:26,085 --> 00:04:26,696
diagram.

62
00:04:27,036 --> 00:04:32,048
Push rulesets. In the documentation's
words: "With push rulesets, you can block

63
00:04:32,088 --> 00:04:37,420
pushes to a private or internal repository
and that repository's entire fork network

64
00:04:37,460 --> 00:04:42,727
based on file extensions, file path lengths,
file and folder paths, and file sizes.

65
00:04:42,767 --> 00:04:47,971
Push rules do not require any branch
targeting because they apply to every push to

66
00:04:48,011 --> 00:04:48,994
the repository."

67
00:04:49,334 --> 00:04:55,407
Four push rules. Restrict file paths: fnmatch
patterns, at most 200 entries of up to

68
00:04:55,447 --> 00:05:01,228
200 characters. Restrict file path length.
Restrict file extensions: at most 200

69
00:05:01,268 --> 00:05:07,340
entries. Restrict file size: a limit in
megabytes, which does not apply to Git Large

70
00:05:07,380 --> 00:05:08,286
File Storage.

71
00:05:08,626 --> 00:05:14,201
Also documented: at most 1,000 ref updates
per push, and the rules apply to the REST

72
00:05:14,241 --> 00:05:18,278
endpoints that create blobs,
trees and file contents as well.

73
00:05:18,318 --> 00:05:23,625
"Allowed exceptions" for paths and sizes
are a preview since the twenty-fifth of

74
00:05:23,665 --> 00:05:26,299
August 2026. Do not depend on a preview.

75
00:05:26,639 --> 00:05:30,334
Two things make push rulesets
different from branch rulesets.

76
00:05:30,374 --> 00:05:35,478
They look at content, not at refs. And forks
inherit them: you heard in the layering

77
00:05:35,518 --> 00:05:40,131
section that forks do not inherit branch
or tag rulesets and do inherit push

78
00:05:40,171 --> 00:05:42,336
rulesets from their root repository.

79
00:05:42,676 --> 00:05:46,880
The plan gate: push rulesets need the
Team plan and a private or internal

80
00:05:46,920 --> 00:05:47,519
repository.

81
00:05:47,859 --> 00:05:53,260
Organization-level rulesets. In one sentence:
an organization ruleset is one ruleset

82
00:05:53,300 --> 00:05:58,637
that targets many repositories, selected
by name pattern, by custom property, or by

83
00:05:58,677 --> 00:05:59,220
a filter.

84
00:05:59,560 --> 00:06:04,688
They are available on Team and Enterprise
plans since the sixteenth of June 2025.

85
00:06:04,728 --> 00:06:09,409
Repositories are targeted as all
repositories, only selected repositories,

86
00:06:09,449 --> 00:06:14,322
repositories matching a name, or, since
the twenty-fourth of June 2025 and by

87
00:06:14,362 --> 00:06:19,426
default, repositories matching a filter.
Only organization owners can edit them.

88
00:06:19,466 --> 00:06:24,785
Repository administrators can add stricter
repository rulesets and cannot weaken the

89
00:06:24,825 --> 00:06:29,953
organization's. Enterprise-level rulesets
add one more layer on Enterprise Cloud.

90
00:06:30,293 --> 00:06:35,457
A caveat the textbook marks unverified:
one sentence of GitHub's "About rulesets"

91
00:06:35,497 --> 00:06:40,468
still says organization rulesets need
the Enterprise plan, while the June 2025

92
00:06:40,508 --> 00:06:45,415
changelog and the organization article
say Team. The course records this as a

93
00:06:45,455 --> 00:06:49,848
conflict inside GitHub's documentation
and prefers the later sources.

94
00:06:50,188 --> 00:06:55,261
Classic branch protection. In one sentence:
a classic branch protection rule is the

95
00:06:55,301 --> 00:07:00,312
older mechanism: one rule per branch-name
pattern, visible to administrators, with

96
00:07:00,352 --> 00:07:05,302
its own bypass behaviour, still supported
and still evaluated alongside rulesets.

97
00:07:05,642 --> 00:07:11,052
The version note: rulesets are the recommended
mechanism since the seventh of July

98
00:07:11,092 --> 00:07:16,569
2026, and since the eleventh of August 2026
a "Convert to ruleset" control migrates

99
00:07:16,609 --> 00:07:21,686
one classic rule at a time. No end date
for classic rules has been announced.

100
00:07:21,726 --> 00:07:25,674
When you inherit a repository,
look for classic rules first.

101
00:07:26,014 --> 00:07:30,958
The differences. How many apply to one
branch: under classic protection, "only a

102
00:07:30,998 --> 00:07:34,135
single branch protection
rule can apply at a time".

103
00:07:34,175 --> 00:07:39,243
Rulesets are all aggregated. Who can see
a classic rule: people with admin access,

104
00:07:39,283 --> 00:07:44,164
whereas "anyone with read access to a
repository can view its active rulesets".

105
00:07:44,504 --> 00:07:49,517
Switching off: you delete the classic rule,
and you change the enforcement status of

106
00:07:49,557 --> 00:07:54,389
a ruleset. Administrators: under a classic
rule, not bound by default, unless "Do

107
00:07:54,429 --> 00:07:57,517
not allow bypassing the
above settings" is selected.

108
00:07:57,557 --> 00:08:02,570
Under a ruleset, bound, unless on the bypass
list. Scope: branches of one repository

109
00:08:02,610 --> 00:08:07,442
for a classic rule, against branches, tags
and pushes at repository, organization

110
00:08:07,482 --> 00:08:08,705
and enterprise level.

111
00:08:09,045 --> 00:08:13,767
Signed commits when a branch is created:
not verified by a classic rule unless

112
00:08:13,807 --> 00:08:18,712
matching-branch creation is restricted.
And the rejection message Git shows for a

113
00:08:18,752 --> 00:08:23,168
classic rule begins remote: error:
GH006: Protected branch update failed.

114
00:08:23,208 --> 00:08:27,564
For rulesets the message is not
documented on the pages the course read.

115
00:08:27,904 --> 00:08:32,632
The administrators row is the hook. And
one fact for the investigation afterwards:

116
00:08:32,672 --> 00:08:36,703
under classic rules the audit log
records an administrator's override.

117
00:08:37,043 --> 00:08:42,233
Conversion. The tool converts one classic
rule at a time, and the new ruleset is

118
00:08:42,273 --> 00:08:43,213
Active at once.

119
00:08:43,553 --> 00:08:48,096
Quick quiz. You convert a classic
rule and keep the old one.

120
00:08:48,136 --> 00:08:52,374
What must a change satisfy
now? A: the new ruleset only.

121
00:08:52,414 --> 00:08:55,964
B: the classic rule only.
C: both. Your answer?

122
00:09:00,804 --> 00:09:05,700
C. If you keep the classic rule, the
documentation says, "an Active ruleset is

123
00:09:05,740 --> 00:09:09,054
enforced alongside it, so
changes must satisfy both".

124
00:09:09,094 --> 00:09:14,307
The conversion covers every protection type
except "Require conversation resolution

125
00:09:14,347 --> 00:09:19,433
before merging". One more caveat marked
unverified: how the classic "Lock branch"

126
00:09:19,473 --> 00:09:22,660
setting maps onto ruleset
rules is not spelled out.

127
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:28,361
Plan gates. These are the facts most likely
to have changed by the time you watch

128
00:09:28,401 --> 00:09:31,628
this. Each was read on the
first of October 2026.

129
00:09:31,968 --> 00:09:36,979
Repository rulesets and classic branch
protection: public repositories on GitHub

130
00:09:37,019 --> 00:09:41,400
Free and Free for organizations, and
public and private on paid plans.

131
00:09:41,440 --> 00:09:46,451
Organization-level rulesets: Team and
Enterprise. Enterprise-level rulesets, the

132
00:09:46,491 --> 00:09:51,503
Evaluate status, metadata restrictions
and ruleset history: the Enterprise Cloud

133
00:09:51,543 --> 00:09:56,491
documentation only. Push rulesets: Team
plan, private or internal repositories.

134
00:09:56,531 --> 00:10:01,417
Merge queue: public repositories owned
by an organization, and private ones on

135
00:10:01,457 --> 00:10:06,342
Enterprise Cloud. Required reviewers by
team: organization-owned repositories.

136
00:10:06,382 --> 00:10:11,520
Rule Insights: Team and Enterprise Cloud.
Auto-merge: public repositories on Free,

137
00:10:11,560 --> 00:10:13,856
and public and private on paid plans.

138
00:10:14,196 --> 00:10:19,281
For you this means one thing: a public
repository in a free practice organization.

139
00:10:19,321 --> 00:10:24,094
There you can create branch and tag
rulesets, bypass lists, required reviews,

140
00:10:24,134 --> 00:10:29,219
required checks and code owner review. A
private repository on a free plan accepts

141
00:10:29,259 --> 00:10:32,032
none of it, which is why
the labs are public.

142
00:10:33,872 --> 00:10:38,499
Think of the protection of a repository
as locks on different doors, fitted in

143
00:10:38,539 --> 00:10:43,346
different years by different people. The
branch door has a new lock, the ruleset,

144
00:10:43,386 --> 00:10:48,313
with a public list of who has a master key.
It may also still have an old lock, the

145
00:10:48,353 --> 00:10:53,220
classic rule, whose master keys were handed
to every administrator by default, and

146
00:10:53,260 --> 00:10:55,255
which only administrators can see.

147
00:10:55,595 --> 00:11:00,475
The tag door may have no lock at all. And
the loading dock, the content of pushes,

148
00:11:00,515 --> 00:11:03,235
is locked only in buildings
on a certain plan.

149
00:11:03,575 --> 00:11:07,745
"The repository is protected" is
therefore not a statement you can check.

150
00:11:07,785 --> 00:11:11,090
"This ref, against this kind
of update, by this actor" is.

151
00:11:11,430 --> 00:11:16,365
The model breaks where rulesets are better
than locks: two locks on one door do not

152
00:11:16,405 --> 00:11:21,399
combine into a stricter lock, but rulesets
and a classic rule on the same branch do.

153
00:11:21,439 --> 00:11:22,957
Changes must satisfy both.

154
00:11:24,797 --> 00:11:28,926
Read the first row. v* matches
v1.0.0 and v2, as intended.

155
00:11:28,966 --> 00:11:34,820
It also matches version-notes, a tag that
is not a release: under your tag ruleset

156
00:11:34,860 --> 00:11:40,714
nobody could move or delete it. And it does
not match release/v1, because the star

157
00:11:40,754 --> 00:11:42,511
does not cross the slash.

158
00:11:42,851 --> 00:11:49,745
The second row, v[0-9]*, requires a
digit after the v: v1.0.0 matches and

159
00:11:49,785 --> 00:11:51,930
version-notes does not.

160
00:11:52,270 --> 00:11:56,027
The lower block repeats what you
saw two videos ago for branches.

161
00:11:56,067 --> 00:12:00,933
The cells marked with the letters n and a
were not run in the course transcripts, so

162
00:12:00,973 --> 00:12:05,839
I do not fill them in from memory. That is
the habit to copy: test a pattern, do not

163
00:12:05,879 --> 00:12:06,774
reason about it.

164
00:12:11,114 --> 00:12:16,537
The same hook as in video 131. This time
read past the refs/heads/main case to the

165
00:12:16,577 --> 00:12:21,800
part that handles tags: it is another
case on the ref name, with rules for refs

166
00:12:21,840 --> 00:12:26,996
under refs/tags/. A tag ruleset is the
same mechanism with a different target.

167
00:12:33,336 --> 00:12:38,447
A new label on the server: a release tag
is created and pushed, one commit behind

168
00:12:38,487 --> 00:12:39,909
main. Creation is open.

169
00:12:40,249 --> 00:12:43,573
Now the supply-chain mistake:
moving a published tag.

170
00:12:43,613 --> 00:12:48,270
git tag -f moves it locally. git push
--force would move it on the server.

171
00:12:48,310 --> 00:12:52,459
The five answers for that push. It
replaces the tag on the server.

172
00:12:52,499 --> 00:12:57,664
It destroys the guarantee that the name
means one commit, for everyone who fetches

173
00:12:57,704 --> 00:13:02,551
from now on. Preview with git ls-remote
--tags origin against your local tag.

174
00:13:02,591 --> 00:13:06,930
Recovery is to push the old tag
object back, if someone still has it.

175
00:13:06,970 --> 00:13:11,246
And it is appropriate essentially
never for a published release tag.

176
00:13:13,586 --> 00:13:18,638
Two ref updates on a protected tag:
a forced move, then a deletion.

177
00:13:18,678 --> 00:13:20,462
Which rule refuses each?

178
00:13:20,802 --> 00:13:25,897
The move is refused by "restrict updates":
the tag already exists and may not move.

179
00:13:25,937 --> 00:13:30,970
The deletion is refused by "restrict
deletions". Both lines say "remote rejected".

180
00:13:31,010 --> 00:13:36,043
Your local tag, by the way, has moved: the
transcript says "Updated tag", with the

181
00:13:36,083 --> 00:13:36,476
old ID.

182
00:13:36,816 --> 00:13:41,947
The server still has the original. Your
clone and the server now disagree about

183
00:13:41,987 --> 00:13:47,381
v1.0.0, and that is yours to repair locally.
Next, the hook is switched off, like a

184
00:13:47,421 --> 00:13:51,832
ruleset set to Disabled. Predict
what the same forced push does now.

185
00:13:51,872 --> 00:13:52,880
Say it out loud.

186
00:13:57,720 --> 00:14:02,328
With enforcement "disabled", the same forced
tag push goes through: "forced update".

187
00:14:02,668 --> 00:14:07,373
One switched-off ruleset, and a published
version name now means a different commit.

188
00:14:12,213 --> 00:14:18,900
A tag ruleset targets v*. Is a
tag named version-notes covered?

189
00:14:18,940 --> 00:14:20,395
Is release/v1?

190
00:14:20,735 --> 00:14:27,529
version-notes is covered, and release/v1
is not. v[0-9]* is the tighter target for

191
00:14:27,569 --> 00:14:28,612
version tags.

192
00:14:28,952 --> 00:14:32,900
Open the repository's settings
and the page that lists rulesets.

193
00:14:32,940 --> 00:14:38,073
From Lab 23.1 you have one branch ruleset
there. Three things to find on this page.

