admin permissions unless "Do not allow

212
00:16:04,151 --> 00:16:06,622
bypassing the above settings" is selected.

213
00:16:06,962 --> 00:16:10,259
Protecting main and leaving
release tags unprotected.

214
00:16:10,299 --> 00:16:15,548
Root cause: branch rulesets target refs/heads/;
a tag is a different ref and needs a

215
00:16:15,588 --> 00:16:16,304
tag ruleset.

216
00:16:16,644 --> 00:16:21,877
Converting a classic rule and keeping it.
Root cause: the new ruleset is Active at

217
00:16:21,917 --> 00:16:26,893
once and is enforced alongside the classic
rule, so changes must satisfy both.

218
00:16:27,233 --> 00:16:32,088
Planning to block large files in a public
repository on a Free organization with a

219
00:16:32,128 --> 00:16:37,102
push ruleset. Root cause: push rulesets need
the Team plan and a private or internal

220
00:16:37,142 --> 00:16:37,759
repository.

221
00:16:38,099 --> 00:16:42,979
Trusting a tag target pattern without
testing it. Root cause: * does not cross a

222
00:16:43,019 --> 00:16:45,869
slash and matches more
names than version tags.

223
00:16:47,709 --> 00:16:52,711
Now, out of the lab. After the force push
in the hook, an ML infrastructure team

224
00:16:52,751 --> 00:16:57,753
answers the CTO's question: they write
down, per ref, what is actually enforced.

225
00:16:58,093 --> 00:17:02,811
For main: one classic rule, administrators
not bound, and since last month a

226
00:17:02,851 --> 00:17:07,944
ruleset. They select the classic setting
that binds administrators as an immediate

227
00:17:07,984 --> 00:17:13,077
fix, then convert the classic rule with
the conversion tool, check with gh ruleset

228
00:17:13,117 --> 00:17:17,897
check main that the resulting ruleset
covers what the old rule did, note that

229
00:17:17,937 --> 00:17:23,093
conversation resolution is the one setting
the tool does not carry over, and remove

230
00:17:23,133 --> 00:17:27,349
the classic rule so that only one
mechanism is left to reason about.

231
00:17:27,689 --> 00:17:34,081
For release tags: nothing. They add a tag
ruleset on v[0-9]* with updates, deletions

232
00:17:34,121 --> 00:17:40,437
and force pushes restricted, and a bypass
entry for the release automation's app on

233
00:17:40,477 --> 00:17:41,509
creation only.

234
00:17:41,849 --> 00:17:47,249
For content: they would like "no *.ckpt,
*.safetensors or *.parquet in Git" and a

235
00:17:47,289 --> 00:17:52,823
size ceiling well below GitHub's hard 100
MiB, so that large files go to Git LFS or

236
00:17:52,863 --> 00:17:58,263
to object storage. That is the textbook's
own example of a push ruleset for an ML

237
00:17:58,303 --> 00:18:03,905
team. Their production repositories are
private on the Team plan, so it is available

238
00:18:03,945 --> 00:18:08,606
to them. In the public practice
organization of this course it is not.

239
00:18:08,946 --> 00:18:13,764
And at the organization level, on their
plan, one ruleset targets every repository

240
00:18:13,804 --> 00:18:18,326
with a given custom property. The textbook
describes that roll-out as a chain

241
00:18:18,366 --> 00:18:23,243
inferred from documented pieces, not as one
documented procedure, and they treat it

242
00:18:23,283 --> 00:18:25,080
that way: one repository first.

243
00:18:26,920 --> 00:18:32,557
Your turn. Do Exercise 23.2, "Which
branches does the pattern cover?", in

244
00:18:32,597 --> 00:18:39,011
exercises/m19-m25-github.md. For every
pattern and every ref name, write "match" or

245
00:18:39,051 --> 00:18:45,077
"no match" before you test anything, and
mark the cells where you were unsure.

246
00:18:45,117 --> 00:18:50,053
Then test the unsure ones with
the match.rb script from labs/run

247
00:18:50,093 --> 00:18:55,963
ch18/fnmatch-targets, and with gh ruleset
check on your practice repository.

248
00:18:56,303 --> 00:18:59,733
The challenge is Exercise
23.8, "Main is protected.

249
00:18:59,773 --> 00:19:03,338
How did a force push get
through?", in the same file.

250
00:19:05,178 --> 00:19:08,355
Question 252 of the CTO question bank:

251
00:19:10,695 --> 00:19:15,303
Answer out loud. A strong answer chooses
differences that change what you check

252
00:19:15,343 --> 00:19:20,186
first when something got through or something
is blocked: who is bound, who can see

253
00:19:20,226 --> 00:19:24,894
the rule, how many rules apply to a branch
and how they combine, and how each is

254
00:19:24,934 --> 00:19:29,601
switched off. For each difference it says
what an investigator does differently.

255
00:19:29,641 --> 00:19:33,484
It mentions that both mechanisms
can be active on the same branch.

256
00:19:35,324 --> 00:19:39,428
Let's land this. "Is it protected?"
is no longer a question you accept.

257
00:19:39,468 --> 00:19:42,112
You ask: which ref, which
update, which actor.

258
00:19:42,452 --> 00:19:44,112
You should now be able to say:

259
00:19:44,452 --> 00:19:49,327
A tag ruleset with updates, deletions and
force pushes restricted keeps a release

260
00:19:49,367 --> 00:19:53,877
tag from moving; test the target pattern,
because * does not cross a slash.

261
00:19:54,217 --> 00:19:59,285
A push ruleset restricts file paths, path
lengths, extensions and sizes for every

262
00:19:59,325 --> 00:20:04,582
push and for the fork network, on the Team
plan in private or internal repositories.

263
00:20:04,922 --> 00:20:10,062
An organization ruleset targets many
repositories; repository administrators can add

264
00:20:10,102 --> 00:20:11,727
to it and cannot weaken it.

265
00:20:12,067 --> 00:20:17,090
A classic rule applies one at a time, is
visible to administrators only, and does

266
00:20:17,130 --> 00:20:21,528
not bind administrators by default;
it is evaluated alongside rulesets.

267
00:20:21,868 --> 00:20:26,388
In a public repository of a free
organization you can practise branch and tag

268
00:20:26,428 --> 00:20:29,527
rulesets; the rest is learned
from the documentation.

269
00:20:31,367 --> 00:20:36,317
Read sections 18.12 to
18.15 of Chapter 18.

270
00:20:36,657 --> 00:20:41,440
That closes the three videos on rules,
and you can now audit a repository's

271
00:20:41,480 --> 00:20:44,721
protection ref by ref.
Practise with Exercise 23.2.

272
00:20:44,761 --> 00:20:48,387
Next: seeing and managing
rules, and "why can't I merge".

273
00:20:48,427 --> 00:20:51,731
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

274
00:20:51,771 --> 00:20:53,275
See you in the next one.
